WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:09.310
<v Speaker 1>Welcome to this deep dive where we explore Reza Shah Kazemi's spiritual ethos and the imam's warning to a governor that pride is war with God.

2
00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:13.470
<v Speaker 1>A war that traces inward to the conscience and outward into mercy.

3
00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:20.630
<v Speaker 1>Pride is war with God and mercy is what justice looks like when a ruler finally remembers who he is.

4
00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:22.910
<v Speaker 1>I mean imagine stepping into a new role.

5
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.710
<v Speaker 1>Maybe you just got a big promotion or you're handed control over a massive project.

6
00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:41.070
<v Speaker 1>And you find yourself looking around the room feeling that quiet, almost invisible shift in your chest, that subtle thought whispering that you are finally where you belong, elevated above the rest, self-made, and just, you know, undeniably superior.

7
00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.390
<v Speaker 1>We barely even register that feeling today.

8
00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:44.510
<v Speaker 2>We just call it confidence.

9
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.990
<v Speaker 1>We do, but we are looking at a profoundly jarring text today.

10
00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:52.830
<v Speaker 1>It's a letter of instruction from the imam, peace be upon him, sent directly to the governor he writes to.

11
00:00:53.110 --> 00:01:01.950
<v Speaker 1>And before this letter touches on tax collection, infrastructure or the daily mechanics of running a society, it drops an anchor that brings the entire concept of leadership to a dead halt.

12
00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:02.750
<v Speaker 2>What does he tell him?

13
00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:05.630
<v Speaker 1>The instruction is a terrifyingly direct command.

14
00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:12.630
<v Speaker 1>He says, do not set your soul up for war with God and just stop and consider the sheer scale of that imagery for a second.

15
00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:14.310
<v Speaker 1>Usually when you and I think of

16
00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:17.230
<v Speaker 1>of war, we picture something physical.

17
00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.070
<v Speaker 2>Clashing armies, drawn swords, shifting borders.

18
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.630
<v Speaker 1>Visible rivals colliding on a battlefield.

19
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.750
<v Speaker 1>But this letter is pointing to something completely invisible.

20
00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:38.590
<v Speaker 1>The Imam is warning that an internal disposition of arrogance, that quiet feeling of superiority you might carry into a meeting, is quite literally an act of open, hostile combat against the absolute.

21
00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:43.550
<v Speaker 2>I don't buy that yet, but how can a private feeling of superiority be called war against God?

22
00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:46.110
<v Speaker 2>It feels like a massive category mistake.

23
00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:53.190
<v Speaker 2>I can understand calling pride a character flaw or seeing how it makes a leader toxic, difficult to work with, or blind to their own mistake.

24
00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:53.950
<v Speaker 1>Sure, that makes sense.

25
00:01:54.030 --> 00:02:04.430
<v Speaker 2>But equating a private internal sense of self-importance with a cosmic militaristic rebellion against the Creator, that sounds like an extraordinary exaggeration.

26
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.870
<v Speaker 2>War requires a rival and God has no rival.

27
00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:18.350
<v Speaker 2>Elevating a bad mood or a moment of vanity to the level of waging war on the absolute sounds like a rhetorical flourish, not a precise spiritual diagnosis.

28
00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:27.710
<v Speaker 1>That skepticism makes complete sense and the text actually addresses it by tearing away the social symptoms of pride to reveal its terrifying machinery because pride is not just a bad mood.

29
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:31.550
<v Speaker 1>For someone wielding power, pride is an act of blatant theft.

30
00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:32.270
<v Speaker 2>Theft?

31
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:33.630
<v Speaker 2>Like what are they stealing?

32
00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:37.710
<v Speaker 1>It is the misappropriation of something that fundamentally does not belong to the human being.

33
00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:38.630
<v Speaker 1>To see the

34
00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:44.350
<v Speaker 1>mechanics of this theft, we have to look at a profound divine saying that forms the beating heart of this teaching.

35
00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:48.590
<v Speaker 1>The saying declares, pride isness, my cloak and greatness is my robe.

36
00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:52.270
<v Speaker 1>Whoever contends with me over either of them, I shall cast him into the fire.

37
00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:55.950
<v Speaker 2>Oh, so we are dealing with the imagery of a sovereign's regalia here?

38
00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:56.830
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

39
00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.790
<v Speaker 1>Let us unpack this image together.

40
00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:02.110
<v Speaker 1>Picture the regalia of a sovereign.

41
00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:09.550
<v Speaker 1>A cloak and a robe in a classical throne room are garments worn exclusively by the king to signify absolute majesty.

42
00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.870
<v Speaker 1>Those garments are never shared.

43
00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.550
<v Speaker 2>Right, they are never lent out to the court.

44
00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:18.350
<v Speaker 1>They are certainly never slipped onto the shoulders of a subject standing in the king's own hall.

45
00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:26.030
<v Speaker 1>When God is proud, it is pure, unadulterated truth because God alone possesses an underived absolute greatness.

46
00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:29.790
<v Speaker 1>But when a human being puts on pride, it is a catastrophic lie.

47
00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:33.710
<v Speaker 2>Because the human being is claiming ownership of something they are only borrowing.

48
00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:35.310
<v Speaker 2>I see the boundary line now.

49
00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:38.510
<v Speaker 2>A creature actually has no inherent greatness to be proud of.

50
00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:42.430
<v Speaker 2>If you are a ruler, every single faculty you possess is on loan.

51
00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:43.470
<v Speaker 1>Every single one.

52
00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.430
<v Speaker 2>The authority you wield is temporarily entrusted to you.

53
00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:55.870
<v Speaker 2>The intellect you use to strategize, the physical energy you use to get out of bed, your very existence from 1 microsecond to the next is being actively sustained by the creator.

54
00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:59.710
<v Speaker 2>To feel pride in those things is a profound delusion of possession.

55
00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:04.030
<v Speaker 1>You are gazing so deeply into the gift that you have entirely forgotten the giver.

56
00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:09.470
<v Speaker 1>And that delusion is exactly what transforms a simple thought into a declaration of war.

57
00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:18.750
<v Speaker 1>By wrapping themselves in the cloak of pride, the ruler is reaching for a garment that belongs exclusively to the sovereign and draping it over their own shoulders.

58
00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.110
<v Speaker 2>So it's a hostile act.

59
00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:24.110
<v Speaker 2>It is a creature attempting to set up a rival throne in the presence of the king.

60
00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:28.190
<v Speaker 1>It is, and this reveals A fascinating paradox about the direction of arrogance.

61
00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:32.910
<v Speaker 1>When you observe a toxic leader, their arrogance usually looks like it is aimed downward.

62
00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:37.790
<v Speaker 1>They look down on their employees, the strong despise the weak, the wealthy mock the poor.

63
00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:41.310
<v Speaker 1>We process that as a horizontal injustice between two people.

64
00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:46.030
<v Speaker 2>But this framework insists that arrogance aimed downward is actually rebellion aimed upward.

65
00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:50.270
<v Speaker 1>Yes, exalting yourself before others is a quiet denial of the sovereignty of the creator.

66
00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:56.670
<v Speaker 1>It is forgetting the reality that dominion belongs entirely to God every single day, not just in some distant afterlife.

67
00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:03.470
<v Speaker 1>When a ruler behaves as though the sovereignty of this day belongs to them, they are spiritually opposing reality itself.

68
00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:06.830
<v Speaker 2>But how does a human mind even justify that leap?

69
00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:09.550
<v Speaker 2>How do we convince ourselves that we own the robe?

70
00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:13.230
<v Speaker 1>To understand that, we have to look at the archetype of the proud soul.

71
00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:20.670
<v Speaker 1>The tradition examines Satan converting a mere difference into a defiance long before it ever infects human politics.

72
00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:25.630
<v Speaker 1>Satan was commanded to prostrate before the newly created human being, Adam.

73
00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:27.550
<v Speaker 2>And the command was crystal clear.

74
00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:30.110
<v Speaker 1>It was, yet Satan refused.

75
00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:33.310
<v Speaker 1>Think about the logic behind that refusal for a second.

76
00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:36.830
<v Speaker 1>What argument could possibly be made to the creator of the universe?

77
00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:44.030
<v Speaker 1>Satan reasoned that because he was created from fire and Adam was created merely from clay, fire is nobler than clay.

78
00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:46.830
<v Speaker 2>He took a difference in material and turned it into a hierarchy.

79
00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:47.310
<v Speaker 1>He did.

80
00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:53.230
<v Speaker 1>He observed a mere difference fire versus clay and converted it into a fundamental claim of superiority.

81
00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:58.270
<v Speaker 1>Then he weaponized that invented superiority into outright defiance of a divine command.

82
00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:01.870
<v Speaker 1>That is the blueprint for every single act of human pride that follows.

83
00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:03.550
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I can see how that maps onto us.

84
00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:05.870
<v Speaker 1>Think about how you or I might operate.

85
00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:06.990
<v Speaker 1>We observe

86
00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:07.710
<v Speaker 1>difference.

87
00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:15.990
<v Speaker 1>Maybe you have a higher salary, a sharper intellect, a better lineage, or a more prestigious political office than the person next to you.

88
00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:25.550
<v Speaker 1>take that difference, convert it into an innate superiority, and use it as justification to override the basic laws of decency, justice, and divine command.

89
00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:30.510
<v Speaker 2>So every arrogant leader walks in the exact procession led by that first refusal.

90
00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:35.710
<v Speaker 1>They do, and the disease does not announce itself with trumpets and a declaration of cosmic war.

91
00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:36.910
<v Speaker 1>It whispers.

92
00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:41.550
<v Speaker 1>slips in disguised as reasonable self-regard or wounded professional dignity.

93
00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:48.510
<v Speaker 2>If a ruler is literally in charge, why is thinking I order and am obeyed, considered corrupting or delusional?

94
00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:52.270
<v Speaker 2>Isn't stating I order and am obeyed just an objective fact of governance?

95
00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:54.190
<v Speaker 2>A governor is appointed to issue orders.

96
00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:56.750
<v Speaker 2>Society only functions because those orders are followed.

97
00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:04.670
<v Speaker 2>I struggle to see how recognizing the basic mechanics of your own administrative authority instantly plunges you into the archetype of satanic defiance.

98
00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:06.750
<v Speaker 1>That is a completely fair pushback.

99
00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:14.990
<v Speaker 2>It just feels like demanding an impossible level of self-effacement from someone whose literal job description is to command.

100
00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.390
<v Speaker 1>The danger is not in the act of commanding.

101
00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:19.790
<v Speaker 1>Society needs administrators.

102
00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:25.150
<v Speaker 1>The lethal danger lies entirely in where the ruler locates the source of their authority.

103
00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:30.830
<v Speaker 1>The specific thought, I order and am obeyed, is pinpointed as the very edge of the cliff.

104
00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:35.550
<v Speaker 1>When a ruler tells themselves this, they are quietly relocating the source of their power.

105
00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:38.990
<v Speaker 2>They stop viewing their power as a heavy trust lent to them by God.

106
00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:43.710
<v Speaker 1>And they start reading it entirely in themselves, in their own charisma, their own leverage, their own genius.

107
00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:47.790
<v Speaker 1>The text is incredibly specific about the sequence of damage this causes.

108
00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:51.550
<v Speaker 1>It corrupts the heart first, and it erodes religion second.

109
00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:56.830
<v Speaker 1>When power goes to a person's head, arrogance functions as a severe intellectual defect.

110
00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:58.030
<v Speaker 2>An intellectual defect?

111
00:07:58.030 --> 00:07:58.590
<v Speaker 1>How so?

112
00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.590
<v Speaker 1>It is an underestimation of the real.

113
00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:08.110
<v Speaker 1>The ruler only overestimates their own tiny sliver of borrowed power because they have completely lost sight of the overwhelming magnitude of the creator.

114
00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.750
<v Speaker 2>The psychological subtlety there is terrifying.

115
00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:16.190
<v Speaker 2>It means tyranny does not start with a massive dramatic decree to harm a population.

116
00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:19.150
<v Speaker 2>It starts with a silent shift in internal posture.

117
00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:21.630
<v Speaker 2>The ruler starts believing their own press.

118
00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.590
<v Speaker 2>They start performing their leadership for the applause of the room.

119
00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:29.070
<v Speaker 1>And that performance is identified as something far more insidious than simple vanity.

120
00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:37.070
<v Speaker 1>The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, warned of a specific danger known as hidden idolatry.

121
00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:42.990
<v Speaker 1>The warning describes this condition as being more hidden than a black ant on a hard rock on a dark night.

122
00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:48.270
<v Speaker 2>When we hear the word idolatry, we tend to picture physical statues, ancient temples, or primitive rituals.

123
00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:52.910
<v Speaker 1>But the hidden idolatry of pretension happens entirely in the boardroom and the mind.

124
00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:56.430
<v Speaker 1>Imagine a leader who enacts A genuinely helpful public policy.

125
00:08:56.430 --> 00:09:04.110
<v Speaker 1>On paper, it looks perfect, but internally, they are only doing it to secure a legacy, to be praised by historians, or to be admired by their peers.

126
00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:07.230
<v Speaker 1>They have set up that human audience as a rival god.

127
00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:10.750
<v Speaker 2>Are rendering their worship and their service to their own reputation.

128
00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:16.910
<v Speaker 2>They might look devout, but their true object of devotion is the admiring gaze of the public.

129
00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:24.590
<v Speaker 1>And the consequence of that hidden idolatry is uniquely devastating because it reframes how we measure success entirely.

130
00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:30.270
<v Speaker 1>If you act solely for the applause of a human audience, you are eventually handed over to that audience.

131
00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:34.350
<v Speaker 1>You get the hollow praise you desperately wanted and absolutely nothing more.

132
00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:39.230
<v Speaker 2>It forces us to confront the terrifying concept of punishment drawn out by degrees.

133
00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:44.510
<v Speaker 2>We have this deep-seated assumption that if someone is succeeding, God must be pleased with them.

134
00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:48.750
<v Speaker 1>But this framework overturns worldly measures of success completely.

135
00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:57.550
<v Speaker 1>Worldly flourishing, an expanding book account, an untouched political career, vanquished enemies can actually serve as the mask of punishment.

136
00:09:57.550 --> 00:10:00.110
<v Speaker 1>It is administered gradually stage upon stage.

137
00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:05.710
<v Speaker 1>A ruler might look out from their balcony, see their treasury overflowing, and assume they are basking in divine favor.

138
00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:09.310
<v Speaker 2>But that very prosperity might be the instrument hardening their soul.

139
00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:11.790
<v Speaker 1>It traps them in a comfortable delusion.

140
00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.630
<v Speaker 1>They remain formally religious, maybe even

141
00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:20.670
<v Speaker 1>You just title among believers, but functionally they are covering over the truth with every arrogant breath.

142
00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:25.230
<v Speaker 1>Their apparent triumph is just the slow, quiet closing of a trap.

143
00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:26.070
<v Speaker 1>So we are looking.

144
00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:37.630
<v Speaker 2>At a war that starts by stealing the divine cloak, moves into the hidden idolatry of seeking human applause, and culminates in a worldly success that is actually a spiritual death sentence.

145
00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:41.550
<v Speaker 1>Which brings us to a massive shift in our vantage point.

146
00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:49.950
<v Speaker 1>We have to trace this war away from the external throne room, away from the applause of the crowd, and follow it into the most intimate, quiet court of all.

147
00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:55.790
<v Speaker 1>Pride is war with God and mercy is what justice looks like when a ruler finally remembers whose he is.

148
00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:57.870
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I see the transition happening here.

149
00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:04.590
<v Speaker 2>To wage war against the absolute on a cosmic scale, the ruler first has to wage war on their own internal landscape.

150
00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:08.510
<v Speaker 2>Before a tyrant can issue an unjust command that harms a single citizen out in the

151
00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:11.630
<v Speaker 2>physical world, an inner witness has to be violently silenced.

152
00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:13.230
<v Speaker 2>We're talking about the conscience.

153
00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:21.310
<v Speaker 1>Yes, and we need to redefine what that word means because modern secular frameworks treat the conscience as just a personal societal rule book.

154
00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:28.030
<v Speaker 1>We tend to think of it as a collection of norms we absorb from our parents, our culture, or our educational background.

155
00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:31.950
<v Speaker 2>Like if you grew up in one culture, you have one conscience and in another a different one.

156
00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:35.950
<v Speaker 1>But this spiritual ethos shatters that modern definition.

157
00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:38.550
<v Speaker 1>Conscience is not generated by society.

158
00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:43.070
<v Speaker 1>Conscience is a divine breath of discernment infused directly into the soul.

159
00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:48.830
<v Speaker 1>It is the very instrument that allows a human being to distinguish wickedness from righteousness in the 1st place.

160
00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:51.870
<v Speaker 1>Your soul did not arrive in this world as a blank slate.

161
00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:53.710
<v Speaker 2>It arrived already inscribed.

162
00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:56.510
<v Speaker 2>inscribed with the primordial covenant.

163
00:11:56.830 --> 00:12:03.150
<v Speaker 2>The text speaks of a moment before physical existence when the soul was brought forth and asked a single question, am I not your Lord?

164
00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:05.870
<v Speaker 2>And the soul testified, yes, verily we testify.

165
00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:09.710
<v Speaker 1>That innate awareness of divine lordship is the root system.

166
00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:13.310
<v Speaker 1>Moral conscience grows out of that spiritual consciousness.

167
00:12:13.390 --> 00:12:17.790
<v Speaker 1>To know right from wrong is literally to remember that ancient promise.

168
00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:27.630
<v Speaker 1>Therefore, whenever you override your conscience, whenever you feel that quiet tug telling you an action is wrong and you do it anyway, you are not just ignoring a cultural norm.

169
00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:30.830
<v Speaker 2>You are going to war with the trace of God within yourself.

170
00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:34.590
<v Speaker 2>You are actively attempting to erase that ancient covenant.

171
00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:36.630
<v Speaker 1>The true mechanics of tyranny.

172
00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:41.590
<v Speaker 1>A tyrant rarely stands up on day one and boldly declares an allegiance to evil.

173
00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:47.030
<v Speaker 1>They begin by systematically training themselves to no longer hear the voice that recognizes the evil.

174
00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:51.990
<v Speaker 1>They suffocate the divine breath decision by decision until it finally stops objecting.

175
00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:53.990
<v Speaker 2>I have to concede this point entirely.

176
00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:57.270
<v Speaker 2>The psychological truth of this framing is impossible to deny.

177
00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:01.350
<v Speaker 2>When you view the conscience as a divine infusion rather than a social construct,

178
00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:04.310
<v Speaker 2>The entire portrait of the tyrant snaps into focus.

179
00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:04.990
<v Speaker 1>It really does.

180
00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:13.350
<v Speaker 2>The outward tyranny a ruler inflects on a subjugated nation is nothing but a macro level projection of a tyranny they have already established within their own mind.

181
00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:23.510
<v Speaker 2>The ruler who proudly declares, I order and am obeyed to a broken population has already spoken that exact same sentence to their own conscience.

182
00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:30.150
<v Speaker 2>They have permitted the part of themselves that the tradition calls the soul that commands evil to seize the inner throne.

183
00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:38.470
<v Speaker 1>And all the burning cities and broken lives that follow in the physical world are just the radioactive fallout of that initial invisible inner coup.

184
00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:45.190
<v Speaker 1>And because the disease begins with an internal coup, the antidote requires A relentless internal counter-offensive.

185
00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:46.710
<v Speaker 2>What does that counter-offensive look like?

186
00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:56.070
<v Speaker 1>The imam provides the governor with the ultimate mechanism for self-reform, and he does not present it as some optional private hobby for the particularly pious.

187
00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:59.350
<v Speaker 1>He presents it as the absolute prerequisite for public justice.

188
00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:00.070
<v Speaker 1>He writes,

189
00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:03.110
<v Speaker 1>If your aspiration is to reform the people, begin with yourself.

190
00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:08.550
<v Speaker 2>Trying to fix a crooked society while your own soul remains bent is identified as the greatest of all faults.

191
00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:11.190
<v Speaker 1>Justice is defined as proper placement.

192
00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:21.030
<v Speaker 1>You cannot properly place the complex affairs of an entire nation if you have not first properly placed your own soul in a posture of profound humility before the creator.

193
00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:24.870
<v Speaker 2>That completely changes the nature of political ambition.

194
00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:31.910
<v Speaker 2>If a soul fancies it can stand on its own, independent of God, it inevitably takes its own desire as its God.

195
00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:33.830
<v Speaker 2>That is the genesis of all oppression.

196
00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:41.670
<v Speaker 1>The only thing that loosens the grip of the soul that commands evil is experiencing the deep, undeniable knowledge of your own nothingness before the real.

197
00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:47.590
<v Speaker 1>Only a soul that is profoundly lowly before God can ever hope to be truly just toward people.

198
00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:53.550
<v Speaker 1>Which brings us to a deeply counterintuitive instruction regarding who a leader should align themselves with.

199
00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:55.110
<v Speaker 2>Who does he say they should align with?

200
00:14:55.190 --> 00:15:01.190
<v Speaker 1>The imam commands the governor to find his approval with the common folk and to actively prioritize the poor over the elite.

201
00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:05.510
<v Speaker 1>If you read that through a modern political lens, it just looks like shrewd populism.

202
00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:07.750
<v Speaker 1>Secure the base because the elite are fickle.

203
00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:11.670
<v Speaker 2>But the instruction insists this is a vital spiritual imperative.

204
00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:16.630
<v Speaker 1>It is grounded in a reality that levels all humanity in a single stroke before God.

205
00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:18.950
<v Speaker 1>Everyone is absolutely destitute.

206
00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:25.630
<v Speaker 1>The immense wealth a ruler commands is merely borrowed against a poverty they share equally with the beggars sleeping at their gate.

207
00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:28.790
<v Speaker 2>So siding with the poor is not a political strategy.

208
00:15:29.270 --> 00:15:31.510
<v Speaker 2>It is aligning yourself with reality.

209
00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:35.910
<v Speaker 2>The poor represent the actual spiritual condition of every human being.

210
00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:41.990
<v Speaker 2>Courting the rich and seeking validation from the elite is buying into the illusion of self-sufficiency.

211
00:15:42.230 --> 00:15:47.590
<v Speaker 1>You see this principle perfectly embodied in an interaction the imam had with the over-strict ascetic.

212
00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:53.990
<v Speaker 1>Picture that person we all know who makes sure everyone in the room is intensely aware of their extreme lifestyle choices.

213
00:15:54.390 --> 00:16:02.070
<v Speaker 1>This ascetic had cut himself off from ordinary life, adopting severe physical deprivations, and he questioned the imam's rough clothing and coarse food.

214
00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:07.350
<v Speaker 2>The ascetic assumed they were kindred spirits, both rejecting the material world for personal purity.

215
00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:12.390
<v Speaker 1>But the imam firmly corrected him, drawing a crucial distinction that changes everything.

216
00:16:12.870 --> 00:16:16.070
<v Speaker 1>The ascetic was denying himself for the sake of private piety.

217
00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:22.950
<v Speaker 1>But the true leader, the imam explained, proportions himself to the weakest members of society out of a binding duty.

218
00:16:23.590 --> 00:16:30.950
<v Speaker 1>The ruler voluntarily lowers himself materially so that the poor never have to feel the unique sting of unshared comfort.

219
00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:35.670
<v Speaker 2>It is the exact same material austerity but driven by a completely opposite spirit.

220
00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:40.310
<v Speaker 1>The ascetic's austerity is turned inward, focused on building his own spiritual resume.

221
00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:47.910
<v Speaker 1>The ruler's austerity is turned outward, functioning as the quiet mortar that prevents a fragile society from cracking under the weight of inequality.

222
00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:50.070
<v Speaker 2>It is an unbelievably high standard.

223
00:16:50.470 --> 00:16:56.870
<v Speaker 2>The ruler refuses the false witchness of the world in order to hold it on the exact same terms as the weakest citizen.

224
00:16:57.430 --> 00:17:00.230
<v Speaker 2>But it requires genuine, agonizing detachment.

225
00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:01.190
<v Speaker 2>You cannot fake this.

226
00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:02.030
<v Speaker 1>No, you can't.

227
00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:13.110
<v Speaker 2>If a modern leader dresses plainly or takes the subway as a calculated piece of political theater while their heart still secretly covets absolute power, they are just performing the hidden idolatry we discussed earlier.

228
00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:15.670
<v Speaker 1>True detachment is the only key that works.

229
00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:21.750
<v Speaker 1>parable from an early Sufi master that perfectly illustrates the mechanics of this self-lowering.

230
00:17:22.310 --> 00:17:25.110
<v Speaker 1>The master approaches the door of the beloved and knocks.

231
00:17:25.430 --> 00:17:28.150
<v Speaker 1>A voice from inside asks, who is there?

232
00:17:28.550 --> 00:17:32.230
<v Speaker 1>The master answers, it is I, and the door stays firmly shut.

233
00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:34.710
<v Speaker 2>So the ego blocks the entrance.

234
00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:41.990
<v Speaker 1>Yes, so the master goes away, undergoes intense spiritual purification, strips away his ego, returns to the door, and knocks again.

235
00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:42.950
<v Speaker 1>Who is there?

236
00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:47.350
<v Speaker 1>This time he answers, it is thou, and only then does the door open.

237
00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:52.150
<v Speaker 2>The door of spiritual reality does not open to an ego that arrives full of itself.

238
00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:55.990
<v Speaker 2>It cannot be forced open by leverage, wealth, or political cunning.

239
00:17:56.470 --> 00:17:58.790
<v Speaker 2>It only yields when the eye has been emptied out.

240
00:17:58.790 --> 00:18:05.750
<v Speaker 1>When a leader achieves that level of emptiness, they finally stop governing from a place of defensive pride and start governing from a place of reliance.

241
00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:11.510
<v Speaker 1>And the natural, inevitable fruit of a life lived in reliance on divine mercy is the outpouring of mercy toward others.

242
00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:16.790
<v Speaker 1>A leader who keenly feels their own perpetual need for forgiveness is a leader who forgives.

243
00:18:17.030 --> 00:18:21.670
<v Speaker 2>That sounds like wordplay isn't calling mercy justice, just renaming softness.

244
00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:28.070
<v Speaker 2>If we are talking about a practical system of governance, justice and compassion usually pull in opposite directions.

245
00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:32.390
<v Speaker 2>Justice is supposed to be stern, objective, and punitive.

246
00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:35.590
<v Speaker 2>Compassion is soft, subjective, and lenient.

247
00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:39.670
<v Speaker 2>You constantly have to balance them against each other in the real world.

248
00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:41.910
<v Speaker 1>I understand why it looks like a contradiction.

249
00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:52.310
<v Speaker 2>Claiming that compassion is an intrinsic dimension of justice just seems like a poetic way of saying a ruler should avoid being too harsh, effectively watering down the strict demands of the law.

250
00:18:52.630 --> 00:18:54.070
<v Speaker 1>It is not wordplay at all.

251
00:18:54.070 --> 00:18:57.270
<v Speaker 1>It is a fundamental redefinition of what justice actually is.

252
00:18:57.830 --> 00:19:02.150
<v Speaker 1>The framework vigorously defends the position that mercy does not soften justice.

253
00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:07.430
<v Speaker 1>Mercy is an intrinsic dimension of justice because it aligns perfectly with the fundamental nature of reality.

254
00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:12.230
<v Speaker 1>Remember, our definition, justice is putting things in their proper place in accordance with the real.

255
00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:13.750
<v Speaker 2>So what is the nature of the real?

256
00:19:13.910 --> 00:19:16.070
<v Speaker 1>Look at the revelation brought by the prophet.

257
00:19:16.150 --> 00:19:21.270
<v Speaker 1>Nearly every single chapter begins by naming God as infinitely compassionate and ever merciful.

258
00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:25.430
<v Speaker 1>Mercy is the very first attribute established about the Creator.

259
00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:30.870
<v Speaker 1>Therefore, possessing A capacity for compassion is an innate affinity with the architecture of existence itself.

260
00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:36.390
<v Speaker 1>Mercy is what justice looks like when it is faithful to the nature of the God whose justice it is.

261
00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:38.870
<v Speaker 2>There is a spiritual arithmetic at work there.

262
00:19:39.430 --> 00:19:45.270
<v Speaker 2>The instruction explicitly states that dispensing mercy to others brings down divine mercy upon yourself.

263
00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:47.110
<v Speaker 2>It operates like a conduit.

264
00:19:47.630 --> 00:19:53.230
<v Speaker 2>When A ruler shows mercy to the vulnerable people beneath him, they open the channel for mercy to flow down from above.

265
00:19:53.230 --> 00:19:57.510
<v Speaker 1>To withhold mercy from a subject is to literally seal your own conduit shut.

266
00:19:57.910 --> 00:20:05.190
<v Speaker 1>It is the height of arrogant delusion to beg for leniency from the absolute while simultaneously crushing the weak in your own domain.

267
00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:07.710
<v Speaker 1>Yet we must be absolutely clear on this point.

268
00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:11.030
<v Speaker 1>Prioritizing mercy does not dissolve rigor or accountability.

269
00:20:11.030 --> 00:20:12.550
<v Speaker 2>How do we know it isn't just softness?

270
00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:17.310
<v Speaker 1>To prove that this is not mere softness, we only have to look at the terrifying

271
00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:21.590
<v Speaker 1>strictness of the Imam's justice whenever the public trust was breached.

272
00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:26.230
<v Speaker 1>The iron of justice is incredibly sharp when it comes to the rights of the people.

273
00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:33.830
<v Speaker 1>Consider the historical account of the Imam bringing a piece of red hot iron close to the body of his own blind brother.

274
00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:35.110
<v Speaker 2>His own brother?

275
00:20:35.270 --> 00:20:35.670
<v Speaker 2>Why?

276
00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:42.310
<v Speaker 1>Simply because his blind brother asked for a slightly larger portion from the public treasury than was his rightful due.

277
00:20:42.790 --> 00:20:47.310
<v Speaker 1>The Imam demonstrated in the most visceral way possible that granting such a request

278
00:20:47.470 --> 00:20:52.230
<v Speaker 1>out of family feeling would be plunging them both into a far more terrible fire.

279
00:20:52.310 --> 00:20:55.430
<v Speaker 2>The severity extends across his entire administration.

280
00:20:55.750 --> 00:21:01.510
<v Speaker 2>We see the thundering remonstrance and the direct threat of the sword directed at his own cousin for misusing funds.

281
00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:11.430
<v Speaker 1>We also read the severe oath-bound warning sent to a deputy governor promising catastrophic punishment for misappropriating even the smallest amount of the community's revenue.

282
00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:18.230
<v Speaker 1>And the ultimate proof of this unbending impartiality is the Imam's declaration regarding the Prophet's own grandsons.

283
00:21:18.430 --> 00:21:19.430
<v Speaker 2>What did he say about them?

284
00:21:19.670 --> 00:21:26.870
<v Speaker 1>He stated unequivocally that even they would receive absolutely no leniency if they dared to misuse the people's property.

285
00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:28.550
<v Speaker 1>The iron is real.

286
00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:32.070
<v Speaker 1>Nepotism wearing the clothes of justice is completely rejected.

287
00:21:32.470 --> 00:21:43.430
<v Speaker 1>But, and this is where the genius of the ethos shines, this terrifying strictness regarding the treasury is immediately paired with a breathtaking instruction regarding the treatment of the subjects themselves.

288
00:21:43.750 --> 00:21:51.270
<v Speaker 2>The instruction is to infuse the heart with mercy, love, and kindness for the subjects, and never to behave toward them like a ravenous beast of prey.

289
00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:57.670
<v Speaker 2>And the justification given for this open hand of mercy is one of the most magnificent statements of human unity ever recorded.

290
00:21:58.310 --> 00:22:03.030
<v Speaker 2>The subjects are described as being of two types, either your brother in religion or your like in creation.

291
00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:04.470
<v Speaker 1>Your like in creation.

292
00:22:04.870 --> 00:22:05.990
<v Speaker 1>Notice what is missing.

293
00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:07.670
<v Speaker 1>There is no third category.

294
00:22:07.750 --> 00:22:15.590
<v Speaker 1>There is no group of human beings, no marginalized faction, no foreign demographic who somehow falls outside the jurisdiction of a ruler's compassion.

295
00:22:15.910 --> 00:22:19.590
<v Speaker 1>We see this manifested perfectly in the story of the blind Christian beggar.

296
00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:20.710
<v Speaker 2>What happens in that story?

297
00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:24.710
<v Speaker 1>The imam is walking through the marketplace and sees this elderly man begging.

298
00:22:25.190 --> 00:22:28.070
<v Speaker 1>He asks about him, learns that society had

299
00:22:28.190 --> 00:22:35.030
<v Speaker 1>employed the man until he was infirm and then abandoned him, and the imam immediately orders his maintenance to be paid from the public treasury.

300
00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:39.830
<v Speaker 1>The man's creed was entirely irrelevant to his claim on the community's mercy.

301
00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:44.230
<v Speaker 1>His shared humanity, his shared creation, was the only metric that mattered.

302
00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:49.790
<v Speaker 2>That approach establishes A principle of social welfare that strictly refuses to discriminate.

303
00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:53.990
<v Speaker 2>It is a stunning historical application of the core philosophy.

304
00:22:54.390 --> 00:23:02.710
<v Speaker 2>The health of a society is not measured by the opulence of its elite or the size of its monuments, but by how it treats the people standing at its absolute weakest edge.

305
00:23:03.270 --> 00:23:09.190
<v Speaker 1>This specific refusal to make a person's creed the measure of their right to mercy has endured as a living thread.

306
00:23:09.590 --> 00:23:12.550
<v Speaker 1>It is central to the classical Ismaili reading of this ethos.

307
00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:20.630
<v Speaker 1>The Aga Khan has emphasized across a lifetime of guidance that faith must prove itself in the compassion it extends to the whole of humanity.

308
00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:27.990
<v Speaker 2>An undiscriminating mercy is the truest indicator of a properly ordered soul and a properly ordered society.

309
00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:37.030
<v Speaker 2>A mercy that does not pause to interrogate a freezing man about which God he prays to before it hands him a coat is the ultimate manifestation of justice.

310
00:23:37.550 --> 00:23:41.670
<v Speaker 1>When you view the entire landscape of this teaching, you see a single, unbroken movement.

311
00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:46.070
<v Speaker 1>It begins at the cosmic heights with the catastrophic theft of the divine cloak.

312
00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:51.590
<v Speaker 1>It descends into the boardroom with the hidden idolatry of pretending to possess underived greatness.

313
00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:55.510
<v Speaker 1>It moves inward, violently suppressing the divine breath of conscience.

314
00:23:55.750 --> 00:24:03.670
<v Speaker 2>But when that terrifying trajectory is reversed through the grueling work of self-reform, the soul empties itself and knocks on the door and says, it is thou.

315
00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:06.790
<v Speaker 2>It lowers itself to stand shoulder to shoulder with the destitute.

316
00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:14.070
<v Speaker 1>From that emptied, lowered position, it finally becomes capable of channeling a mercy that mirrors the compassionate nature of the real.

317
00:24:14.790 --> 00:24:21.270
<v Speaker 1>Pride is war with God, and mercy is what justice looks like when a ruler finally remembers whose he is.

318
00:24:21.990 --> 00:24:34.710
<v Speaker 1>We have traced this quiet war from the theft of divine regalia down into the embattled corridors of human conscience, out into the severe iron of public accountability, and finally into the open hands feeding a blind beggar.

319
00:24:34.950 --> 00:24:36.390
<v Speaker 2>The sequence is unbreakable.

320
00:24:36.790 --> 00:24:39.590
<v Speaker 2>The arrogant soul lies to itself about what it owns,

321
00:24:39.830 --> 00:24:44.070
<v Speaker 2>silences the inner witness that tries to warn it, and inevitably devours the weak.

322
00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:53.270
<v Speaker 1>But the humble soul remembers its own staggering poverty, listens closely to the ancient covenant echoing in its conscience, and extends a mercy that encompasses all of creation.

323
00:24:53.590 --> 00:25:02.310
<v Speaker 1>It brings us to a final, lingering question, one that applies far beyond the walls of any palace and reaches into the daily reality of every person listening right now.

324
00:25:02.710 --> 00:25:06.950
<v Speaker 2>Which voice inside you have you learned not to hear and what would listening to it cost you this week?
